---
title: Notes on「The Luxury of Children」
date: 2026-11-11
author: Jesús Fernández-Villaverde
draft: false
---
*From [Foreign Affairs](https://www.foreignaffairs.com/united-states/luxury-children)*

> The story of the coming decades is not simply one of dwindling and aging. There is a great deal of variation in the baby bust

什么是baby bust？baby bust是相对baby boom（”婴儿潮“）来讲的概念，没有明确的翻译共识，或许可称为婴儿荒

> Last year, humanity’s total fertility level dropped below what demographers call the replacement rate—that is, the average number of children that a woman must have to keep a population constant over the long run.
> 
> But now, simply as a function of their choices, humans will see the world population peak around 2055 and then begin to fall.

或许我们会好奇，为什么跌破更替水平之后不会立马达到峰值，就像物理的加速度为零那样？因为这里的统计尺度是按年的，但概念尺度是按代的。一代大致是25—30年，低于更替水平告诉我们的是下一代的人口会小于这一代。

> Once an economic necessity, children are now an economic luxury.

The core，just copy it。

> Take Mexico. According to the most recent survey, in 2024, a Mexican woman could expect to have 1.2 children over her lifetime, well below the 1.57 average of non-Hispanic white women in the United States.

什么是non-Hispanic white？在美国联邦统计体系中，种族（Race）和族裔（Hispanic Origin）可以交叉，特意强调非西班牙，其实也就是独立取出欧洲裔美国人这个群体，来提升群体特征的显著性。西班牙裔美国人的社会地位和收入较低。

> Or take another favorite culprit, smartphones. Some researchers have suggested that the spread of the smartphone stoked atomization and a desire for individual fulfillment, discouraging many people from having children. But fertility rates have been plunging worldwide since the late 1960s, well before smartphones appeared, and there is little evidence of an acceleration in the drop after their introduction. Instead, the device is more likely an accelerator of underlying mechanisms than a cause in itself: by my calculations, smartphones compressed into ten years a change in fertility that would have occurred anyway over 25.

这里其实有些歧义，因为压缩在数字上也体现为加速。然而作者这里的意思是，不应该认为手机是“culprit”（罪魁祸首/根本原因），导致生育率降低的社会机制，其存在并不依赖于手机，而手机只是加快了整体的社会时间，从而让既有社会机制本来就应该促成的结果提前了（”compressed “）。

> Indeed, across OECD countries, the correlation between fertility, on the one hand, and female education and employment, on the other, is positive

其实这个结果还是相当自然的，或许是因为我作为一个现代公民来思考吧，感觉旧的框架（women have chosen to prioritize other goals instead of child rearing）反而显得更加反常识。

> Modernity raises the cost of children and lowers the cost of childlessness. Parents must equip children with formal knowledge and credentials, an endeavor that takes decades of effort and spending. Tight career schedules also conflict with parents’ biological clocks.

感同身受啊，虽然是从Child的视角（？）

> Many adults still want to have children and flourishing families. When Gallup asked Americans about their ideal number of children in July 2025, the average answer was 2.7, a figure that has barely changed since the 1970s. But children are costly and bring no income. They have become something of a luxury good that richer people can afford more of than poorer people can

相当关键的一点insight.人们的观念其实并没有变，繁荣的家庭仍然是ideal，但这里已经不是想不想的问题，而是能不能（"afford"）的问题。这极具说服力地反驳了很多传统观点（观念化的解释）。

如果怀疑普遍性，原研究是”对居住在美国50个州及哥伦比亚特区的、年满18岁的普通成年人，通过电话进行访谈，电话号码通过随机数字拨号（RDD）方式选取“，朴素但有效。

> Stable families lead to higher fertility (and much better outcomes for the children). In short, income and education soften some of the worst consequences of modernity.The already sharp divide between regions and social groups with higher average incomes and education levels and those with lower average incomes and education levels will grow more pronounced.

正如布尔迪厄认为，文化资本（表现为教育资质、语言能力、文化品味等）与经济资本（收入）是可以相互转化并共同运作的。“现代性”往往带来不稳定、异化和风险。高学历和高收入群体能够购买更好的医疗保障、教育资源和居住环境，拥有对“什么是好的养育方式”下定义的权力。
